course: dictionary
lesson: 13677
title: Qualities that belong to something
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe a quality that is part of a thing itself, so deeply that it cannot be taken away. That is what inherent means. It describes something natural and basic, a part of the thing from the start.
explain: «Fire has an inherent danger: it can burn you.» The danger is part of fire itself. You cannot remove it, you can only be careful. Compare this with «The bike has a bell fixed to the handlebar», where the bell is added on, so it is not inherent in the bike.
explain: Ask yourself: is this quality part of the thing, or added from outside? If it is part of the thing, inherent may fit. Check by swapping in your meaning: «There is a natural danger in fire» still makes sense, so you have the right idea.
fact: Inherent means natural and basic, a part of something that cannot be taken away.
fact: «Inherently» is the adverb: «Puppies are inherently playful.» This means playfulness is part of what puppies are.
fact: «There is an inherent risk in riding a bike.» The risk comes with riding itself.
fact: Inherent is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in books, exams and science, not in everyday chat.
fact: In science, a property inherent in a material is a quality the material has naturally, such as the strength of steel.
exercise: «Fire has an inherent danger: it can burn you.» What does inherent mean here? | Hidden under the ground | *Natural, a basic part of fire that cannot be taken away | Added on later by someone | Very bright
exercise: Ben says, «The shine is inherent in the gold.» What does he mean? | *The shine is a natural part of the gold | The shine is in the box, not the gold | The shine comes and goes with the weather | The shine was painted on later
exercise: Which sentence shows a quality that is inherent? | The cup was moved to the table | Mum added extra salt to the soup | The bike was painted red last week | *Sugar is naturally sweet, and sweetness is part of sugar itself
test: «Swimming in the sea has an inherent risk.» What is inherent about the risk? | Painted on by someone | It disappears when you dry off | *It is a natural part of swimming in the sea | It only happens on Tuesdays
test: Which word is the adverb of inherent? | *inherently | inherit | inherited | inheritance
exam: «Diamonds are inherently hard.» Which meaning fits best? | Hard to find in shops | *Hard as a natural, basic quality that belongs to the diamond itself | Hard only when it is cold | Hard because someone made them so later
